.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:18:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:19:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:20:# Manager constructed iter2 revision brief from memory instead of re-reading the locked spec verbatim
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:21:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:22:## What went wrong
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:23:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:24:The iter2 Preparation leader brief listed 8 H2 sections that contradicted the locked Idea Design A at `ideation/staging/design/item-a-codex-skill-structure.md` lines 15-23. Specifically:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:25:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:26:- The brief said H2 #8 was "Constraints" — but the locked spec has "Anti-patterns" as H2 #8.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:27:- The brief omitted "Cost + sandbox budget awareness" as H2 #7 entirely.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:28:- The brief instructed frontmatter key `when-to-load` — but the canonical project skill convention is `allowed-tools` (16 of 16 existing skills use `allowed-tools`; 0 use `when-to-load`).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:29:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:30:The leader followed the brief faithfully. The resulting iter2 stub diverged from the locked Idea contract at exactly 2 structural points (section 7 substitution + frontmatter shape), causing REVISE verdicts from both Claude and Codex evaluators.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:31:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:32:## Why
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:33:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:34:The manager constructed the iter2 revision brief from memory — recalling "the locked 8 sections" imperfectly — rather than `Read`-ing `item-a-codex-skill-structure.md` lines 15-23 into context before authoring. The mistake originates from the iter1 Codex evaluation itself, which also miscalled `when-to-load` as the correct frontmatter. The manager inherited that incorrect finding and propagated it as a "fix-mandate" in the iter2 brief without cross-verifying against the Ideation contract.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:35:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:36:This is an Iron Law 7 (Principle 7) violation: "NO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE." The verification gate was applied to the leader's output (evaluators checked the stub against the spec) but not to the manager's own brief-construction step. The manager assumed memory was sufficient for encoding a "paste-inline-verbatim" specification.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:37:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:38:## How to recognize
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:39:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:40:Before the manager authors any delegation brief that contains "exact" or "verbatim" instructions (e.g., "use exactly these 8 H2 sections in this order"), check whether the locked spec was `Read` into context within the last 10 manager turns of the current session. If the spec has not been freshly read, that is the warning sign.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:41:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:42:Specific triggers:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:43:- Brief contains phrases like "the locked sequence is..." or "use exactly these sections..."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:44:- The section names or field names in the brief differ by even one word from the locked spec
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:45:- The brief cites a prior evaluator's description of the spec rather than the spec itself
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:46:- The brief references line numbers but those lines have not been scrolled through in-session
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:47:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:48:## Corrected approach
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:49:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:50:The manager MUST `Read` the locked spec into context immediately before constructing any "paste-inline-verbatim" section of a delegation brief. The `Read` call serves as the verification gate — without it, the brief is a memory claim, not a verified specification.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:51:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:52:Companion rule: when a delegation brief includes any verbatim content claim, the brief-construction turn should grep-cite the source line range (e.g., "per `item-a-codex-skill-structure.md:15-23`") and that grep must have run in the current session. A citation that was not freshly verified is not a citation — it is a paraphrase.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:53:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:54:Concrete fix for this mistake class:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:55:1. Before authoring the revision brief, `Read` the locked spec file at the cited line range.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:56:2. Copy the exact text from the `Read` output into the brief — never retype from memory.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:57:3. Include the file path + line range as an inline citation in the brief so evaluators can re-verify independently.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:1:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:2:slug: manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:3:title: "Manager rm -rf'd worktree .gobbi/ chain without verifying tracked files — Iron Law 1 violation"
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:4:domain: process
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:5:severity: high
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:6:mistake-candidate: true
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:7:project: gobbi
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:8:session: 2026-05-22-bac669ad-4fec-40b5-8387-51ac57bc0d3d
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:9:loop: wrap-up
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:10:created: 2026-05-22
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:11:status: active
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:12:supersedes: null
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:13:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:14:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:15:# Manager rm -rf'd worktree .gobbi/ chain without investigating tracked files
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:16:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:17:## What went wrong
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:18:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:19:During cleanup of the Codex session-write-path violation (see companion mistake `codex-eval-session-write-path-nested-in-worktree`), the manager ran `rm -rf` on the `.gobbi/` directory chain inside the Execution worktree (`.gobbi/projects/gobbi/worktrees/feat/env-var-audit-sessionstart-hook/.gobbi/`) to remove the misplaced session files.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:20:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:21:The `.gobbi/` chain at that location was not just a stale sandbox artifact — it also contained **tracked files** that belonged to the branch HEAD (the worktree was on `feat/env-var-audit-sessionstart-hook`, and some `.gobbi/` content was committed on that branch). The `rm -rf` silently deleted tracked files without a warning, because `rm -rf` does not consult git. The manager only discovered the deletion when the subsequent `git status` showed the tracked files as deleted.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:22:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:23:Recovery was performed via `git restore .gobbi/` in the worktree, which re-checked-out the tracked files from HEAD. No permanent data loss occurred. The incident was recorded in the Preparation decisions log (finding γ adjunct note).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:24:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:25:This is a direct violation of **Iron Law 1 (NO ACTION WITHOUT THINKING IT THROUGH FIRST)** — specifically, the manager did not verify what was in the directory before deleting it.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:26:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:27:## Why it went wrong
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:28:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:29:The manager's mental model was: "this `.gobbi/` tree inside the worktree is an artifact of the Codex sandbox's incorrect write — it shouldn't be there, so I can safely remove it." The assumption missed that a git worktree shares the same `.git` tracking as the main tree. Files committed on the branch are present in the worktree by design — the worktree IS a checked-out branch. A `rm -rf` inside a worktree can delete tracked files just as easily as inside the main tree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:30:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:31:The missing step was a `git status` + `git ls-files` check before the deletion.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:32:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:33:## How to recognize
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:34:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:35:- You are about to run `rm -rf <path>` on any path inside a git worktree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:36:- The `<path>` includes directories that could plausibly hold git-tracked files (any directory tracked by the repo — e.g., `.gobbi/`, `.claude/`, `packages/`, `src/`).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:37:- The motivation is "this is an artifact that shouldn't be here" — but you have not verified it via git.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:38:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:39:## Corrected approach
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:40:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:41:Before any `rm -rf` on a path inside a git repository (main tree or worktree):
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:42:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:43:1. **Run `git -C <worktree-root> status --short <path>`** — if the path shows any tracked files (no `??` prefix), those files are tracked and `rm -rf` will produce deleted entries in the index.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:44:2. **Run `git -C <worktree-root> ls-files --error-unmatch <path>` (or `git ls-files <path>`)** to enumerate exactly which files inside the path are tracked.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:45:3. **Only proceed with `rm -rf` if the path contains ZERO tracked files** (i.e., all entries are untracked `??` in `git status`).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:46:4. If tracked files are present and the intent is to remove only untracked artifacts: use `git clean -fd <path>` to remove untracked files while leaving tracked files intact. Add `-n` (dry run) first to verify the set of files that would be removed.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:47:5. If the intent is to remove tracked files as well, stage the deletion via `git rm -r <path>` rather than `rm -rf` — this produces a clean deletion record in the index.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:1:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:2:date: 2026-05-24
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:3:session: 1b26cf20-677b-498c-8c1b-7d7e971597ac
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:4:status: accepted
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:5:feature: session-foundations-bundle-b
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:6:mistake-candidate: true
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:7:domain: process
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:8:project-scope: true
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:9:supersedes: null
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:10:superseded_by: null
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:11:finding-id: WRAP-MIST-003
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:12:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:13:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:14:# Bundles with ≥8 plan tasks risk manager-context overflow mid-Execution
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:15:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:16:## Context
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:17:Prior session 2026-05-23-7ea62d36 emergency-stopped mid-Execution at Task 02 iter2 due to manager context overflow. This session resumed Bundle B (10 tasks + iter2 cycles + dual-system evals) and observed the same pressure curve: large per-task subagent reports + ITER cycles + dual-system evals + multiple file reads accumulate quickly. We finished only by aggressive batching (T07+T08 shared executor; pruning Codex evals on small fixes) and trimming reads.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:18:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:19:## Decision
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:20:Bundles of ≥8 plan tasks should be scoped against the manager's context budget at Planning time. Two levers:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:21:1. **Split the bundle across sessions** when total parallel-spawn weight (estimated tokens × tasks × iter cap × dual-system) exceeds budget.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:22:2. **Adopt single-system evals on small iter2 fixes** (matcher rename, 1-char typo, footnote insertion). Dual-system only for substantive iter1 reviews.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:23:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:24:## Rationale
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:25:- Iron Law 11: context budget is a real resource; not budgeting for it is "no improvement that games the tool" applied to plan size.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:26:- The emergency-stop is unrecoverable mid-Execution and forces an extra session for resume + Wrap-up (this session itself is proof).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:27:- Dual-system on every iter (including trivial fixes) is the literal example of "metric improvement at high cost" — single-system catches the same issues for small fixes.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:28:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:29:## Alternatives considered
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:30:- Always plan ≤ 6 tasks. Rejected — sometimes scope demands more, and rigid caps are gaming. Heuristic-based decision is better.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:31:- Spawn-thread the manager (subagent-of-subagent). Rejected — orchestration boundary already says one manager, and parallel state coordination is fraught.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:32:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:33:## Consequences
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:34:- Planning loop adds a "manager-context-budget" check: estimate budget vs. plan size; if at risk, split.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:35:- Eval-protocol matrix: dual-system on iter1 (high-stakes); optional single-system on iter2 surgical fixes (low-stakes).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:36:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:37:## How to recognize next time
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:38:- Plan task count ≥ 8 AND projected dual-system × ITER cap > 3 per task.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:39:- Mid-Execution manager context > 80% used before half the tasks are done.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:40:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:41:## Corrected approach
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:42:At Planning EVALUATION (or Ideation Lock Scope when tasks count is known): if ≥ 8 tasks with ≥ 2-iter ceiling each, route the second half to a follow-up session via backlog entry. If the user insists on single-session, switch iter2+ to single-system eval by default.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:43:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:44:## Related
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:45:- Prior session HANDOFF (emergency-stop) at `sessions/2026-05-22-7ea62d36-.../HANDOFF.md` (or equivalent path)
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:46:- This session's HANDOFF.md (resume + completion)
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:1:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:2:date: 2026-05-24
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:3:session: 1b26cf20-677b-498c-8c1b-7d7e971597ac
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:4:status: accepted
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:5:feature: session-foundations-bundle-b
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:6:mistake-candidate: true
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:7:domain: process
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:8:supersedes: null
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:9:superseded_by: null
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:10:finding-id: WRAP-MIST-001
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:11:---
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:12:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:13:# Worktree-mode executors must edit worktree-internal canonical mirror, not main-tree canonical mirror
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:14:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:15:## Context
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:16:In worktree mode (the canonical mode this session shipped via T1), the worktree's `.gobbi/projects/{project}/skills/...` files are **physical copies on the branch**, not symlinks back to the main tree. Multiple executors this session were briefed with the older guidance "edit the canonical mirror at `.gobbi/projects/...`" without absolute paths, then proceeded to edit the main-tree mirror (because their cwd resolved there) — leaving the worktree branch untouched. Task 03 surfaced this explicitly: the executor reported editing main-tree canonical, then had to revert + re-apply inside the worktree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:17:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:18:## Decision
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:19:Worktree-mode delegation prompts MUST specify the canonical path as the **worktree-absolute** path (e.g., `<worktree>/.gobbi/projects/{project}/skills/{skill}/SKILL.md`), not the bare `.gobbi/projects/...` form. The executor's `cd` (or absolute path use) must be inside the worktree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:20:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:21:## Rationale
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:22:- The whole point of worktree mode is branch-isolated changes; editing the main-tree copy defeats it.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:23:- The Edit-tool symlink-refusal mistake (`edit-tool-refuses-symlink-canonical-fallback.md`) already pushes executors to the canonical-mirror path — without absolute disambiguation the fallback path is ambiguous between worktree and main tree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:24:- Reverting + re-applying is fragile; the fix is preventive in the brief.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:25:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:26:## Alternatives considered
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:27:- Trust the executor to `cd` into the worktree first. Rejected — multiple briefs this session showed executors editing main-tree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:28:- Add a post-Execution diff-check gate in orchestration. Possible follow-up but does not prevent the wrong edit; only catches it.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:29:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:30:## Consequences
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:31:- Manager delegation prompts in worktree mode need a "Worktree-canonical paths" block calling out the absolute path.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:32:- Phase-doc per-iter commit cadence rule (T05) is unaffected — it already references `worktreePath`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:33:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:34:## How to recognize next time
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:35:- Brief uses bare `.gobbi/projects/...` for an executor without an explicit absolute path or a `cd "$worktreePath"` instruction.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:36:- Executor's verification command reads from main-tree rather than `git -C <worktree> show HEAD:<path>`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:37:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:38:## Corrected approach
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:39:Add a "Canonical-mirror paths (worktree-absolute)" subsection to executor briefs in worktree mode, listing every file the executor may touch as `<worktree>/.gobbi/projects/{project}/skills/...`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:40:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:41:## Related
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:42:- `sessions/2026-05-23-1b26cf20-677b-498c-8c1b-7d7e971597ac/execution/task-01/staging/decisions/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-canonical-fallback.md`
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:43:- T1 implementation (PR #269)
